---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { DATA_YEAR, occupations, states } from '../lib/salary';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Salary Methodology: How We Calculate Every Number"
  description="Exactly how SalaryByCity produces its figures: BLS OEWS source data, percentile definitions, state adjustment methods, cost-of-living context, update schedule, and known limitations."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Salary Methodology</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Last reviewed: January {DATA_YEAR}</p>

    <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      Every number on SalaryByCity can be traced to a public, government source and a documented
      calculation. This page describes that pipeline end to end — because a salary statistic you
      can't audit is just a rumor with a chart.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">1. Primary data source</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      Our wage data is based on the <strong>Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS)</strong>
      program of the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics (<a href="https://www.bls.gov/oes/" class="text-blue-700 underline dark:text-blue-400" rel="noopener">bls.gov/oes</a>).
      OEWS is a semiannual survey of roughly 1.1 million business establishments — the most
      comprehensive occupational wage measurement in the United States. Each occupation is
      identified by its Standard Occupational Classification (SOC) code, which we display on every
      occupation page so you can verify our figures against the BLS website directly. We retrieve
      the data programmatically through the <a href="https://www.bls.gov/developers/" class="text-blue-700 underline dark:text-blue-400" rel="noopener">BLS Public Data API</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">2. What the percentiles mean</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      Every salary page shows five points of the annual wage distribution. The <strong>10th
      percentile</strong> means 10% of workers in that occupation and state earn less — a realistic
      entry-level figure. The <strong>median (50th percentile)</strong> is the midpoint: half earn
      more, half earn less. We lead with the median rather than the mean because a handful of very
      high earners can drag an average far above what a typical worker sees. The <strong>90th
      percentile</strong> approximates senior, top-of-market pay. BLS "top-codes" annual wages above
      roughly $239,200; where that occurs (some physicians, lawyers, and executives), the true top
      decile is higher than shown, and we note it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">3. How state figures are produced</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      Where BLS publishes an exact state-level wage distribution for an occupation, we use it
      directly. For a minority of state-and-occupation combinations, BLS suppresses data to protect
      survey confidentiality. In those cases we estimate the distribution by applying a
      <strong>state adjustment factor</strong> — derived from the state's overall wage level relative
      to the national average, refined by industry mix — to the occupation's national percentiles,
      rounded to the nearest $10. Estimated figures are always labeled "modeled on" BLS data rather
      than presented as survey results. Current figures are modeled on the May 2024 OEWS vintage,
      adjusted to {DATA_YEAR}.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">4. Cost-of-living and tax context</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      Cost-of-living indexes (U.S. average = 100) reflect composite state price levels for housing,
      groceries, utilities, transportation, and healthcare. The "purchasing power" figure on each
      page divides the state median salary by the state's index — a standard real-wage adjustment.
      State income tax summaries reflect statutory rates in effect for {DATA_YEAR}; they are context
      for comparing states, not tax advice.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">5. Update schedule</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      BLS releases new OEWS estimates once a year, typically each spring. We refresh our dataset
      with each release, re-verify state tax and cost-of-living context at the same time, and
      rebuild every page. The "last reviewed" date at the top of this page reflects the most recent
      full review.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">6. Known limitations</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      OEWS measures base wages — it excludes most bonuses, equity, overtime, and self-employment
      income, which materially understates total compensation in fields like finance, law, sales,
      and software. Statewide figures also mask metro-level variation: pay in a state's largest
      city usually runs above its statewide median. Finally, statistics describe the market, not
      your offer. We currently publish {occupations.length} occupations × {states.length} states;
      if you spot an error, please
      <a href="/contact/" class="text-blue-700 underline dark:text-blue-400">tell us</a> — corrections ship in days, not quarters.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
